<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Vikrama Rajasinha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri Vikrama Rajasinha was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, reigning from 1798 to 1815, of the South Indian Nayak dynasty. His harsh rule alienated the Kandyan nobility, who conspired with the British. A British force entered Kandy in 1815, he was deposed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandyan-convention">Kandyan Convention</a> and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-vikrama-rajasinha.jpg" alt="Sri Vikrama Rajasinha" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vikrama_Rajasinha_Kandy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Kannasamy Nayak, he was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a> and the nephew of Sri Rajadhi Rajasinha, whom he succeeded in 1798 at the age of eighteen. The Nayak kings were of Telugu origin, practised Shaivite Hinduism and were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>; they used Tamil as a court language alongside Sinhala. Queen Upendrama's brother Muthuswamy had a stronger claim to the throne, but the first Adigar, or chief minister, Pilimatalauwa chose the young prince, reportedly planning to seize the crown for himself in time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Pilimatalauwa worked secretly with the British, who held the coastal provinces, to provoke the king into a war that would give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> a cause for intervention. On 22 March 1803 the British entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> unopposed after the king had fled; in June the Adigar massacred the British garrison and restored him. Pilimatalauwa's plot to take the throne was later discovered, and, having been pardoned twice before, he was executed.</p><p>His nephew and successor Ehelepola Nilame fell under the same suspicion. After a rebellion he instigated was put down he fled to the British, and the king confiscated his lands and ordered the imprisonment and execution of his wife and children. Ehelepola persuaded the British that the king's tyranny justified intervention, and the seizure and torture of British merchants, several of whom died, gave the pretext. The invading force reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> without resistance on 10 February 1815, and the kingdom was ceded on 2 March. The historian Louis Edmund Blaze judged him less a monster of cruelty than a weak tool of scheming chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Held at Vellore Fort on a small British allowance with his two queens, he died of dropsy on 30 January 1832. Kandy Lake and the Octagon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-tooth">Sri Dalada Maligawa</a>, built in 1802 by the royal architect Devendra Mulachari, were made on his instructions. His royal standard, a yellow lion holding a sword on a red ground, is part of the present flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and his tomb near Vellore is the memorial Muthu Mandapam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sri Vikrama Rajasinha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent among Kandyan nobles against <b>Sri Vikrama Rajasinha</b> allowed British forces to enter Kandy in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Vellore Fort, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.92,78.12&amp;year=1813" aria-label="Madurai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6704 1793 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sri Vikrama Rajasinha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.92,78.12&amp;year=1813">What was here in 1813? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
